Or maybe you want to take everything with you?

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Towing can help.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Whether it's pulling, lugging, yanking, hooking or hoisting,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
being a drag was never so good.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, towing on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,600
This is the easy part.

10
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:49,780
This is the hard part.

11
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:57,940
And it's not just this.

12
00:00:58,760 --> 00:00:59,520
It's this.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
And this.

14
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:05,000
And even this.

15
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:11,000
You may think you know towing, but there's an incredible variety to it.

16
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:14,000
A whole science to it.

17
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:17,000
And sure enough, a history.

18
00:01:17,001 --> 00:01:23,000
But a good place to start is on the side of the road, with your car inoperable,

19
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:28,000
and tow truck operator Rusty Morse on his way in one of his state-of-the-art trucks.

20
00:01:28,001 --> 00:01:36,800
Most of the towing done nowadays is done with the wheel lift due to the fact that it's mostly damage free.

21
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,340
Many of the vehicles today are too soft to be lifted up by the bumpers of the front end,

22
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,740
so you have to pick them up by the tire so no damage can occur.

23
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:51,300
It may be nerve-wracking for a driver to watch his car being hauled away on the end of a truck,

24
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:53,960
but it rarely does any damage to the vehicle.

25
00:01:53,961 --> 00:01:59,960
As you can see, the L-arm cradles the tire quite nicely.

26
00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:03,960
No part of the vehicle is touched during the towing process.

27
00:02:03,961 --> 00:02:05,960
We retract the vehicle.

28
00:02:05,961 --> 00:02:14,613
We want to put as much weight forward as we can and still leave room enough between the vehicle and the back of our tow truck to turn safely without hitting the bar on

29
00:02:14,614 --> 00:02:15,960
the back of the tow truck.

30
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:22,960
This tow truck is a sturdy workhorse, capable of picking up vehicles weighing thousands of pounds.

31
00:02:22,961 --> 00:02:28,078
of pounds. But what if you've just driven your big rig into a ditch?

32
00:02:28,079 --> 00:02:29,960
Operators like Rusty have

33
00:02:29,961 --> 00:02:33,960
that covered too, with state-of-the-art monsters that can handle anything that's road-worthy.

34
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:40,200
With the rotator boom, I have the option to work off the side of the truck as opposed to working

35
00:02:40,201 --> 00:02:46,680
off the back, which allows me to not have to close lanes of the freeway, especially in heavily

36
00:02:46,681 --> 00:02:49,980
congested areas.

37
00:02:49,981 --> 00:02:53,440
Operators may need to brace their trucks with outriggers like these that

38
00:02:53,441 --> 00:02:59,440
stabilize their vehicles against the weight of their tow.

39
00:02:59,441 --> 00:03:05,960
When I pick up heavy loads, I have to watch the chassis tilt to see if it starts to rise up.

40
00:03:05,961 --> 00:03:11,000
If it does, then I have to set the load back down and reposition my truck where I can extend

41
00:03:11,001 --> 00:03:14,580
my outriggers out a little bit.

42
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:17,160
In a way, this is how the auto towing industry began.

43
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:23,140
In a ditch, nearly a century ago in 1916, in Chattanooga, Tennessee,

44
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:25,620
with a man named Ernest Holmes.

45
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:27,910
Ernest Holmes Sr.

46
00:03:27,911 --> 00:03:32,680
was considered the grandfather of the towing and recovery industry.

47
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,580
There was a car wreck here in Chattanooga with 10 Lizzy,

48
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,520
and it went off into Chickamauga Creek.

49
00:03:40,521 --> 00:03:46,020
It took Holmes and ten men just to pull out the tin lizzie.

50
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:53,700
He said there's got to be an easier way than this to get a wreck out of the creek.

51
00:03:53,880 --> 00:04:00,480
And so he went back to his garage and started working on and formulating a plan to build a wrecker.

52
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:04,800
Holmes had been pondering an additional dilemma.

53
00:04:04,801 --> 00:04:10,920
Mechanics who had all their best tools in their shops had to leave most of them behind

54
00:04:10,921 --> 00:04:17,800
to brave the elements at some secluded breakdown where a stranded customer required service.

55
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:22,900
To solve both problems, Holmes designed a recovery vehicle from a 1913 Cadillac auto

56
00:04:22,901 --> 00:04:28,860
chassis, cut off behind the driver's seat, with a tripod of poles welded to the frame

57
00:04:28,861 --> 00:04:32,900
to create a crane.

58
00:04:32,901 --> 00:04:39,160
On the poles was a winch or pulley system that provided mechanical advantage.

59
00:04:39,161 --> 00:04:43,220
Mechanical advantage can allow a single person to do what might normally take several people

60
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:44,220
to do.

61
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:51,060
For instance, a pulley might enable a single person to lift a weight ten feet.

62
00:04:51,061 --> 00:04:55,460
The pulley may need twenty feet of rope for that lift, but one person alone can move it

63
00:04:55,461 --> 00:04:57,340
with the pulley.

64
00:04:57,341 --> 00:05:02,640
The formula is work equals force times distance.

65
00:05:02,641 --> 00:05:05,900
the towing industry would later rely on proved to be on Holmes' wrecker.

66
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:12,080
But the first time he took it out, the truck failed, and he had to pull the wreck out with

67
00:05:12,081 --> 00:05:13,240
old-fashioned manpower.

68
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:17,555
He didn't have the outriggers set on it yet.

69
00:05:17,556 --> 00:05:20,680
That's the device that's let down on the side

70
00:05:20,681 --> 00:05:21,920
to stabilize it.

71
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:27,140
Correction, almost everything was on that first truck.

72
00:05:27,141 --> 00:05:30,980
Holmes added the outriggers before job number two

73
00:05:30,981 --> 00:05:34,600
and the 15 billion dollar American towing industry

74
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:38,480
created in the wake of Mr. Holmes' first unsuccessful recovery

75
00:05:38,481 --> 00:05:42,400
has been in a sense a continual refinement of those

76
00:05:42,401 --> 00:05:43,440
original elements

77
00:05:43,441 --> 00:05:52,760
early on, Holmes seized the opportunity to sell his homemade tow trucks

78
00:05:52,761 --> 00:05:56,320
to repair shops and began making wreckers full time

79
00:05:56,321 --> 00:06:06,320
Though he was joined by other manufacturers, Holmes remained an innovator throughout the 20s.

80
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:16,180
Most of the time they used an old car, a heavy-duty car, like a Cadillac or a locomobile or something like that,

81
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:20,100
and cut the back of it out and built a wrecker on the back of it.

82
00:06:20,101 --> 00:06:29,880
The tow truck has basically involved from an A-frame hoist on the back of a vehicle with just a block and tackle or a chain hoist.

83
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:41,080
It has grown into having two winch cables to work with, which was one of Ernest Holmes' early innovations, and made it a lot easier for recovery.

84
00:06:41,081 --> 00:06:50,680
Two winch cables allow a single truck using chains to tie off to both the wreck and an anchor post, like a rock or a tree.

85
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,120
Early towing equipment hooked into the frame or steel skeleton of the vehicle to be towed.

86
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:05,140
Holmes' pivoting boom allowed tow trucks to recover vehicles from the side or at difficult angles.

87
00:07:05,141 --> 00:07:15,260
Towers also used a retractable cradle or ambulance that slid under a wreck and lifted part or all of it off the road.

88
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:23,000
During the 30s, motorized winches powered off the truck's transmission began to overtake the hand-cranked ones.

89
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:30,100
The mechanical wreckers pulled to something either give or broke, which was usually the wire rope.

90
00:07:30,101 --> 00:07:37,600
A flailing rope, one that could potentially decapitate someone, underscores another constant in the towing business.

91
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,160
It's dangerous.

92
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:49,140
Operators who weren't killed by a snapped rope were careful not to be crushed by a damaged automobile or hit by oncoming traffic.

93
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:55,120
Today, towing equipment is designed to cease operating before reaching a breaking point.

94
00:07:55,121 --> 00:08:03,280
But back in the 20s, the growing industry coincided with explosive auto sales and the attendant

95
00:08:03,281 --> 00:08:10,480
rash of breakdowns, collisions and other mishaps.

96
00:08:10,481 --> 00:08:14,760
Eventually, manufacturers realized that towing wasn't just a way to get wrecks back to the

97
00:08:14,761 --> 00:08:17,520
shop.

98
00:08:17,521 --> 00:08:21,880
Towing was its own industry, separate from auto repair.

99
00:08:21,881 --> 00:08:25,280
In light of this, they stopped cobbling trucks together from spare parts.

100
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:30,460
They started buying new trucks, which are called chassis,

101
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:34,500
and they would take and order the chassis,

102
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:37,500
and then they would build the wreckers to go on the back of it.

103
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:39,660
So that really changed the industry.

104
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:44,760
Tow truck manufacturers now sold brand new wreckers

105
00:08:44,761 --> 00:08:46,580
that were specifically designed for towing.

106
00:08:46,581 --> 00:08:53,580
The only significant design changes were new methods for working old ideas.

107
00:08:53,581 --> 00:08:57,580
Although tow truck equipment remained constant,

108
00:08:57,581 --> 00:09:03,580
the way tow truck operators recovered vehicles underwent a major change during the 1970s.

109
00:09:03,581 --> 00:09:11,580
Automakers responding to the energy crisis and demands for safer passenger vehicles

110
00:09:11,581 --> 00:09:16,860
vehicles made lighter, more flexible cars that would be damaged by a traditional frame-hooked

111
00:09:16,861 --> 00:09:17,860
tow.

112
00:09:17,861 --> 00:09:22,849
Newer vehicles aren't strong enough to be towed the old style.

113
00:09:22,850 --> 00:09:24,860
We have plastic fairings,

114
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:32,460
license plates, bumper brackets that could get damaged while towing with a sling.

115
00:09:32,461 --> 00:09:37,260
For instance, that sexy road-hugging design that makes a car attractive to drivers makes

116
00:09:37,261 --> 00:09:39,260
it problematic to tow operators.

117
00:09:39,261 --> 00:09:47,440
As the clearance of automobiles keeps getting lower, we're challenged to keep designing

118
00:09:47,441 --> 00:09:53,620
our equipment to maintain a lower profile to still be able to get underneath it and pick

119
00:09:53,621 --> 00:09:54,620
it up.

120
00:09:54,621 --> 00:09:59,800
Although winches and cranes and outriggers didn't change much, they were now powered by

121
00:09:59,801 --> 00:10:04,860
hydraulics, which are stronger and lighter than the engine-driven machinery, and which

122
00:10:04,861 --> 00:10:11,860
used liquid pumped into a chamber to force the extension and retraction of supports.

123
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:17,140
Modern tow trucks today are basically all hydraulic operations.

124
00:10:17,141 --> 00:10:18,980
You have recovery booms

125
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:25,980
that will extend and raise and lower hydraulically that aid in many recovery situations.

126
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:33,700
Those modern tow trucks are put together here at Miller Industries, just outside of Chattanooga.

127
00:10:33,701 --> 00:10:37,460
the largest tow truck manufacturer in the business.

128
00:10:37,461 --> 00:10:40,740
And like all the others, it still buys chassis ready-made,

129
00:10:40,741 --> 00:10:44,200
then constructs a tow truck behind it.

130
00:10:44,201 --> 00:10:47,940
We use standard steel, but we also use high-yield steels.

131
00:10:47,941 --> 00:10:52,760
We also use a lot of aluminum, and we're into stainless, because weight is an issue

132
00:10:52,761 --> 00:11:00,500
as far as what the vehicles can carry and also what the operator has to do as far as lifting.

133
00:11:00,501 --> 00:11:13,660
Building a 70-ton tow truck with a pivoting hydraulic boom that can rotate a full 360 degrees and can lift, well, another 70-ton tow truck begins here.

134
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:19,880
I think the hardest part is logistics of moving all the individual piece parts through the plant.

135
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:27,040
We manufacture roughly around 5,000 to 6,000 vehicles in the full range from small record carrier and large annually.

136
00:11:27,041 --> 00:11:35,880
The 200,000 square foot plant brings in steel every day, as some 400 people work to assemble the trucks.

137
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:41,260
Workers maneuver the giant parts using overhead cranes in the ceiling.

138
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:46,820
Roughly a month to a month and a half later, out pops a shiny new truck.

139
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:51,980
What you're seeing right here is one of the latest innovations.

140
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:54,620
It's called wireless proportional controls.

141
00:11:54,621 --> 00:11:57,180
basically this wrecker flies by wire.

142
00:11:57,181 --> 00:12:01,860
This allows the operator to take the passenger side controls out of the wrecker itself

143
00:12:01,861 --> 00:12:05,340
and operate the machine up to 500 feet away.

144
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:08,700
Signed, sealed, and delivered.

145
00:12:09,260 --> 00:12:12,960
A truck like this with all the bells and whistles might cost $400,000.

146
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,620
You can order one with wireless proportional controls directly from Miller

147
00:12:18,621 --> 00:12:22,880
or review the different makes and models at one of the industry's conventions.

148
00:12:22,881 --> 00:12:28,100
There you'll find many of the new innovations being introduced into the towing market

149
00:12:28,101 --> 00:12:30,760
to make life easier and safer for operators.

150
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:34,680
And that's where we're going next.

151
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:40,660
It's a tower's paradise at the Towing Trade Show, where all the latest gadgets get together.

152
