and it's got a fully leather upholstered interior, automatic transmission, air conditioning, CD player, all the comforts of home.

172
00:14:18,040 --> 00:14:20,000
It's like Disneyland for towing professionals.

173
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:26,740
A wonderland of new gizmos and improved favorites for the thousands of people who make this their business.

174
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:33,060
The industry actually has towing specific companies of about 50,000

175
00:14:33,061 --> 00:14:36,840
and that means those are companies whose primary business income is towing.

176
00:14:36,841 --> 00:14:43,840
This event will draw 5,000 attendees, including a surprising mix of vendors.

177
00:14:43,841 --> 00:14:52,519
This technology is the sliding technology where the crane that you see behind you slides up and down the chassis of the truck, which enables you to get a lot more

178
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:53,220
reach.

179
00:14:53,221 --> 00:14:56,840
We deal with the regulatory environment because they're dealing with motor vehicles.

180
00:14:56,841 --> 00:15:02,840
Motor vehicles are regulated by states and federal government, and as a result there's paperwork and processes that they have to go through.

181
00:15:02,841 --> 00:15:05,840
Important, but not too exciting.

182
00:15:05,841 --> 00:15:11,680
For thrills, you can join the boys at Dynamic Manufacturing, who are playing with their rotating carrier.

183
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:17,540
It turns 180 degrees. 90 degrees one direction, 90 degrees the other.

184
00:15:17,980 --> 00:15:23,800
And what that is, it basically gives the opportunity to, if a person is pulling a car out of a parking lot,

185
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:29,180
a person is pulling a car out of a ditch, very easy to get to that vehicle.

186
00:15:29,181 --> 00:15:36,180
And if that doesn't do it for you, someone's bound to be hoisting a truck in the air.

187
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:45,180
With their flashy lights and new hydraulics, towing trade shows represent the cutting edge.

188
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:50,180
But the history of towing reaches all the way back into pre-history,

189
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:54,180
to the first time someone pulled an object that needed moving.

190
00:15:54,181 --> 00:16:01,180
Adding animals such as horses and oxen to the equation really put things in motion.

191
00:16:01,181 --> 00:16:08,180
The first vehicle that animals used in work was a sled.

192
00:16:08,181 --> 00:16:15,180
And that was with runners on the ground and they would pull that and that would move an object.

193
00:16:15,181 --> 00:16:22,180
Eventually sled runners were replaced by the wheel, one of the simple and revolutionary machines.

194
00:16:22,181 --> 00:16:28,160
Now, otherwise immovable objects could be easily and efficiently moved from where they

195
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:32,860
happened to be to where they needed to be.

196
00:16:32,861 --> 00:16:36,420
The key was hooking or harnessing the object to the animal.

197
00:16:36,421 --> 00:16:42,440
A device called a yoke, which originated in the fourth millennium BC, fits like an adjustable

198
00:16:42,441 --> 00:16:50,900
collar around the necks of oxen or cattle in front of the bony humps on their backs.

199
00:16:50,901 --> 00:16:57,900
The cattle push against the collar to drive the wagon or plow.

200
00:16:57,901 --> 00:17:04,900
Horses required a lighter device that could be strapped to their bodies because yokes tend to cut off their windpipes.

201
00:17:04,901 --> 00:17:10,900
Horses, like cattle, pulled the carts or wagons by pushing against a harness.

202
00:17:10,901 --> 00:17:13,900
This is the way all animals tow.

203
00:17:13,901 --> 00:17:17,900
And this towing worked quite well for thousands of years.

204
00:17:17,901 --> 00:17:27,200
Before the invention of the internal combustion engine, transportation work was dependent upon the horse and upon steers for work.

205
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:34,360
Large draft horses were bred for the heaviest work, which often involved towing farm equipment.

206
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:41,520
Conversely, miners used small beasts to burden, such as ponies and donkeys.

207
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:46,760
For centuries, the animals had been used to tow ore out of mines.

208
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:55,160
But with the invention of the steam engine, the horse was decisively moved from a place

209
00:17:55,161 --> 00:18:02,560
of vital importance to human technology to one of vastly diminished importance.

210
00:18:02,561 --> 00:18:08,600
Locomotive engines propelled trains across continents, car engines animated the automobile,

211
00:18:08,601 --> 00:18:14,520
and engines propelled ships, which ultimately led to the need for another great towing vehicle,

212
00:18:14,521 --> 00:18:15,520
the tugboat.

213
00:18:15,521 --> 00:18:22,520
Next, the portable powerhouse that runs the waterways.

214
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:27,520
The most famous animal towers on Earth are the Anheuser-Busch Clydesdales.

215
00:18:27,521 --> 00:18:32,520
At over 200 strong, they are the world's largest herd of draft horses.

216
00:18:32,521 --> 00:18:38,520
Towing will return on Modern Marvels.

217
00:18:38,521 --> 00:18:45,520
In October of 2000, four Yemeni harbor tugboats escorted the USS Cole out of the port of Aden.

218
00:18:45,521 --> 00:18:50,520
Though a tugboat escort is the norm for such large ships in harbor areas,

219
00:18:50,521 --> 00:18:53,520
nothing was normal about this escort.

220
00:18:53,521 --> 00:18:57,520
The Cole had a 40-foot square hole in its midsection,

221
00:18:57,521 --> 00:19:01,520
the result of a bomb planted by an Al-Qaeda terrorist cell

222
00:19:01,521 --> 00:19:05,520
that hobbled the ship and killed 17 sailors.

223
00:19:05,521 --> 00:19:12,279
sailors. The harbor tugs transferred the ship to the dry dock deck ship Blue Marlin.

224
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:12,980
The

225
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:20,000
billion-dollar destroyer was then brought back to the United States for repairs. But

226
00:19:20,001 --> 00:19:25,580
tugboats provide assistance to any ship in need, not just the cold, not only in times

227
00:19:25,581 --> 00:19:31,965
of crisis. Dwarfed by their gargantuan payloads, tugboats are deceptively powerful.

228
00:19:31,966 --> 00:19:32,666
Tugs are

229
00:19:32,667 --> 00:19:34,371
perceptively powerful.

230
00:19:34,372 --> 00:19:39,580
Tugs are essentially mobile, independent engines that push, pull

231
00:19:39,820 --> 00:19:44,303
and nudge objects that can't entirely control themselves.

232
00:19:44,304 --> 00:19:46,820
They're usually escorting a ship

233
00:19:46,821 --> 00:19:50,820
much larger than they are safely into a narrow port or berth.

234
00:19:50,821 --> 00:19:57,820
From the tugs perspective you are really at a disadvantage because all you see is a huge

235
00:19:57,821 --> 00:20:03,700
a huge halt of a ship, I kind of feel sorry for the operators on the tugs because they

236
00:20:03,701 --> 00:20:07,500
really can't see what they're part of.

237
00:20:07,501 --> 00:20:12,860
Chad McCauley, chief mate on a Crowley tugboat in Los Angeles Harbor, is off on just such

238
00:20:12,861 --> 00:20:13,860
a job.

239
00:20:13,861 --> 00:20:20,500
This vessel here is the BT Alaska, and she's an old tanker down from Valdez, coming into

240
00:20:20,501 --> 00:20:22,840
Long Beach to discharge a load.

241
00:20:22,841 --> 00:20:26,300
And primarily what we do is ensure that they get to dock safe.

242
00:20:26,301 --> 00:20:30,300
We normally have a pilot boarding area which is about a mile and a half, two miles out.

243
00:20:30,301 --> 00:20:35,300
The port pilot gets aboard and at that point he meets up with two of our escort tugs.

244
00:20:35,301 --> 00:20:39,300
The pilot communicates with those vessels and they tether up.

245
00:20:39,301 --> 00:20:45,300
Our deck wench down there has an eight inch high-tech line and they pull up to the ship,

246
00:20:45,301 --> 00:20:49,300
men on board the ship, pull it aboard, attach it to the deck.

247
00:20:49,301 --> 00:20:53,300
At that point we're attached and we can control the ship in case of an emergency.

248
00:20:53,301 --> 00:21:02,800
Why would a 1,000-foot-long, 188,000-ton ship need help sailing into a 45-foot-deep, 600-foot-wide harbor?

249
00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:07,780
They're just too big, too much mass, it's confined spaces.

250
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:12,460
Their ability to stop is very limited due to the fact that they're in water.

251
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:18,940
But not if Chad and his fellow tugboat operators get into formation around it.

252
00:21:18,941 --> 00:21:25,940
This formation, called a matrix, is based on the tonnage of the vessel and its cargo,

253
00:21:25,941 --> 00:21:31,940
and it determines the amount of horsepower that will be needed to bring the ship slowly and safely into birth.

254
00:21:31,941 --> 00:21:39,940
It also allows the tugs to stop, or even back up, in order to control the behemoth as it moves into harbor.

255
00:21:39,941 --> 00:21:47,940
It seems like they're going really slow, but at this speed, if he hit the dock, he wouldn't stop until he went 300 feet into it.

256
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:53,940
Chad's boat alone can provide 4,800 horsepower.

257
00:21:53,941 --> 00:22:02,940
This modern tugboat operates with a modest four-man crew, two operators and two deckhands, and lots of gadgetry.

258
00:22:02,941 --> 00:22:10,940
Basically here we have our VHF radios for communication with other vessels and with the pilot.

259
00:22:10,941 --> 00:22:16,940
We have a radar, our steering system right here, which consists of these levers and wheel.

260
00:22:16,941 --> 00:22:19,800
and basically the controls for the winch.

261
00:22:21,720 --> 00:22:25,620
Another panel reports information back to the operator about the winch.

262
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:27,880
First one here, this is line tension.

263
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:31,960
This amount of tension and tonnage that we're pulling on the line,

264
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:34,120
there's a meter on the winch.

265
00:22:35,420 --> 00:22:39,580
And scope or footage, amount of line that we actually have off the winch,

266
00:22:40,060 --> 00:22:41,440
82 feet of line at the moment.

267
00:22:42,360 --> 00:22:46,840
But the real workhorse on this tug is the omnidirectional rotary drive system,

268
00:22:46,940 --> 00:22:50,720
that descends from the center of the hull and spins like an eggbeater.

269
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:56,140
Today they can go in any direction you want.

270
00:22:56,240 --> 00:22:57,720
They can thrust sideways.

271
00:22:58,040 --> 00:23:01,020
They can do just about everything but stand on their heads.

272
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:08,780
Once the Crowley Tugs have the BT Alaska safely into its berth,

273
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:13,220
they'll repeat the process five or six more times in a single 12-hour shift.

274
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:26,660
Roughly 6,000 tugboats and towboats serve coastal and inland U.S. waterways, helping

275
00:23:26,661 --> 00:23:33,720
navigate cargo along more than 11,000 miles of riverways, serving 600 ports.

276
00:23:33,721 --> 00:23:42,380
Add to that the cargo shipped on the Great Lakes and a domestic ocean trade.

277
00:23:42,381 --> 00:23:47,720
Nearly all of this trade is possible because of tug and tow boats, which trace their origins

278
00:23:47,721 --> 00:23:58,060
over 200 years, when ships began being built to unwieldy sizes.

279
00:23:58,061 --> 00:24:03,060
The first boat ever built to tie onto and guide other ships was William Symington's

280
00:24:03,061 --> 00:24:09,420
steam-powered 10-horsepower paddle boat Charlotte Dundas in 1802, which made its maiden and final

281
00:24:09,421 --> 00:24:14,060
voyage through the 4th and Clyde Canal in Scotland.

282
00:24:14,061 --> 00:24:19,880
Canal owners fearing its wake would cause erosion took the Charlotte Dundas off the canal.

283
00:24:19,881 --> 00:24:24,500
But the tugboat concept proved unsinkable, and before long waterways were filled with

284
00:24:24,501 --> 00:24:26,420
the small but muscular paddle boats.

285
00:24:26,421 --> 00:24:35,220
It's kind of an obvious use in an era when boats were rowed or sailed or mule drawn.

286
00:24:35,221 --> 00:24:42,320
By the 18, late 50s, early 60s, they'd moved into propeller drive.

287
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:49,800
Working together or alone, they positioned vessels into narrow or shallow areas,

288
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:52,620
pulled foundering vessels into deeper waters,

289
00:24:53,380 --> 00:24:56,380
or transported large barges laden with heavy burdens.

290
00:24:57,460 --> 00:25:03,800
Much like today, tugs of old used strong chains or tow lines to attach themselves to their vessels.

291
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:12,620
And like the auto-towing industry, once the basics were in place, innovation has been a product of refinement and efficiency.

292
00:25:13,680 --> 00:25:16,780
Take the tugboat Hercules, built in 1907, as an example.

293
00:25:18,100 --> 00:25:19,974
Her engine is very typical.

294
00:25:19,975 --> 00:25:26,500
A triple expansion steam engine was really in its own in the late 19th and early 20th century.

295
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:30,840
It was rated about 1,000 horsepower, which was very powerful for its time.

296
00:25:30,841 --> 00:25:36,520
And it got the most use out of this steam, which was about 180 pounds per square inch coming from the boiler.

297
00:25:37,100 --> 00:25:42,080
The boiler really being key in terms of having an efficient pressure to use in a triple expansion engine,

298
00:25:42,400 --> 00:25:44,440
which expanded the steam three times.

299
00:25:45,920 --> 00:25:51,280
Each piston connected to the others turned to crank connected directly to the propeller shaft.

300
00:25:52,840 --> 00:25:54,380
No complicated gears.

301
00:25:54,940 --> 00:25:57,360
Just a simple, efficiently low-speed prop.

302
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:05,840
The bow angles up very sharply.

303
00:26:05,841 --> 00:26:09,840
This is mostly for a practical reason, to gain additional freeboard,

304
00:26:09,841 --> 00:26:13,840
distance between the main deck and the water, to allow her to handle heavy storms

305
00:26:13,841 --> 00:26:15,840
and the waves in those storms.

306
00:26:15,841 --> 00:26:19,840
A side effect of this deck is the grace in the ship's appearance.

307
00:26:19,841 --> 00:26:25,840
Smaller, more efficient diesel tugs eventually replaced the old steam tugs,

308
00:26:25,841 --> 00:26:29,840
beginning in the mid-20s, which reduced the size of crews.

309
00:26:29,841 --> 00:26:36,840
Today, single tugboats run on between 8 and 12 cylinders at 70 to 1,000 revolutions per minute.

310
00:26:36,841 --> 00:26:41,840
Steel hulls have replaced wooden ones.

311
00:26:41,841 --> 00:26:47,840
Stacks and deck houses are shorter to lower the ship's center of gravity.

312
00:26:50,840 --> 00:26:56,840
Towboats are like a freshwater cousin to the tugboat, used to push barges along inland waterways.

313
00:26:56,841 --> 00:26:58,840
waterways.

314
00:26:58,841 --> 00:27:03,600
Tow boats are not ocean going vehicles, but are almost exclusively used for barge transportation

315
00:27:03,601 --> 00:27:07,820
on inland waterways, like the Mississippi.

316
00:27:07,821 --> 00:27:15,420
We typically push 15 barges, and each barge carries approximately 1,500 tons in each barge,

317
00:27:15,421 --> 00:27:21,640
which equates to 60 trucks or 16 rail cars.

318
00:27:21,641 --> 00:27:25,880
So we keep quite a few trucks off the road.

319
00:27:25,881 --> 00:27:29,300
Chirradino Incorporated makes towboats just outside of Chattanooga.

320
00:27:30,420 --> 00:27:35,360
Not surprisingly, these towboats use cables and hydraulic winches to secure their barges.

321
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:43,300
And they have an average of between 700 and 1,500 horsepower, but can be as much as 10,000 horsepower.

322
00:27:44,060 --> 00:27:46,220
Again, historic consistency is the rule.

323
00:27:46,760 --> 00:27:51,240
The boat we're building right now, this is the 45th in the series, this switchboat you're going to see.

324
00:27:51,600 --> 00:27:53,340
And we just, we haven't changed it much at all.

325
00:27:53,341 --> 00:27:57,000
If you see our boat somewhere, you've seen it.

326
00:27:57,380 --> 00:27:58,540
You'll always recognize it.

327
00:28:00,980 --> 00:28:05,960
Tugboats often help less maneuverable ships get safely through tight spaces like canals.

328
00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:09,720
But they aren't the only towing vehicle that does that job.

329
00:28:10,920 --> 00:28:17,920
Next, a specially designed cog railway helps ships sail from the Atlantic Ocean to the Pacific and back again,

330
00:28:19,500 --> 00:28:22,500
tugging massive cargo ships through the Panama Canal.

331
00:28:23,340 --> 00:28:32,700
Jonathan Hulls patented his design for a steam tugboat in 1736, almost a century before

332
00:28:32,701 --> 00:28:37,660
the construction of the first practical tug.

333
00:28:37,661 --> 00:28:41,740
Towing will return on Modern Marvels.

334
00:28:41,741 --> 00:28:49,020
It's this pure, unadulterated thrill-seeking that brings you to the amusement park, to

335
00:28:49,021 --> 00:28:53,820
the lofty heights of the roller coaster.

336
00:28:53,821 --> 00:28:56,140
Thrill seeking motivates you to get on.

337
00:28:56,141 --> 00:28:59,540
But what actually gets you up there?

338
00:28:59,541 --> 00:29:01,540
A good toe.

339
00:29:01,541 --> 00:29:05,620
You crest the highest peak.

340
00:29:05,621 --> 00:29:14,040
Then plummet crazily down the track, swerving maniacally around hairpin turns.

341
00:29:14,041 --> 00:29:16,620
You've got other things to worry about at the time.

342
00:29:16,621 --> 00:29:22,400
But whenever you ride roller coasters like this one at Lake Winnipesoka in Georgia, you can thank a tow chain.

343
00:29:26,320 --> 00:29:31,780
In order to get the car to the top of the hill, we have to tow it by means of a 75 horsepower motor.

344
00:29:32,220 --> 00:29:37,380
The chain is threaded around pulleys, which provide the torque that we need to pull the cars,

345
00:29:37,381 --> 00:29:42,680
which can weigh between four or five tons fully loaded, up the 20 degree grade to the drop point.

346
00:29:46,620 --> 00:29:53,960
The cars of a typical roller coaster, large or small, engage a chain that continuously

347
00:29:53,961 --> 00:29:59,980
runs underneath the first hill by means of hinged hooks, called chain dogs, that hang

348
00:29:59,981 --> 00:30:02,880
below the bottom of the cars.

349
00:30:02,881 --> 00:30:06,940
The chain is wrapped around a motorized pulley system.

350
00:30:06,941 --> 00:30:12,060
The dogs hook into the chain as it runs like a conveyor belt up the hill.

351
00:30:12,061 --> 00:30:17,240
Once the car reaches the initial peak, the chain dogs disengage from the chain, and the

352
00:30:17,241 --> 00:30:31,080
cars are off, hurling along the track from the momentum of that first fall.

353
00:30:31,081 --> 00:30:36,280
If roller coasters are one of the most exciting tow trains in action, then the train mules

354
00:30:36,281 --> 00:30:41,360
at the Panama Canal are one of the most unique.

355
00:30:41,361 --> 00:30:45,800
These powerful trains are mechanical descendants of the horses and mules that once towed ships

356
00:30:45,801 --> 00:30:52,740
through canals.

357
00:30:52,741 --> 00:30:58,540
In Panama, the canal was built with locomotives in mind.

358
00:30:58,541 --> 00:31:03,580
These electric locomotive mules guide large ships through the canal locks.

359
00:31:03,581 --> 00:31:10,540
When a ship approaches a lock, it is brought to a complete stop, then tethered to the engines

360
00:31:10,541 --> 00:31:12,380
that straddle the lock on either side.

361
00:31:14,220 --> 00:31:16,840
You know, if they crash one of those lock gates,

362
00:31:17,540 --> 00:31:19,800
that canal is done until the gate is fixed.

363
00:31:21,900 --> 00:31:24,800
Moving at speeds not faster than 5 miles per hour,

364
00:31:25,640 --> 00:31:27,960
the trains safely navigate the canal locks.

365
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:33,020
The locomotives must climb 85 feet above sea level

366
00:31:33,021 --> 00:31:35,800
and down again over the steep grades and angles

367
00:31:35,801 --> 00:31:37,120
of the canal lock system.

368
00:31:37,121 --> 00:31:40,600
as the ships pass up and down through the locks.

369
00:31:43,660 --> 00:31:50,680
The canal recently upgraded their 26 locomotives at a cost of roughly $2 million for each brand new engine.

370
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,300
But these trains are special in other ways.

371
00:31:55,060 --> 00:32:00,600
These master towers are cog engines, and the cogs fit into a rack in the center of the railroad tracks.

372
00:32:00,601 --> 00:32:09,200
They're modeled on a railway nearly 150 years old, more than 2,000 miles away, and designed

373
00:32:09,201 --> 00:32:23,201
not to pull ships through the Panama Canal, but to push tourists up a mountain.

374
00:32:23,880 --> 00:32:29,040
I was told by one of the railway men that a man came up at the time the Panama Canal

375
00:32:29,041 --> 00:32:32,740
was being built to look at this rack on this railroad.

376
00:32:33,700 --> 00:32:38,880
And he told him that they were thinking of using this type of rack in the locks.

377
00:32:41,920 --> 00:32:45,760
This railroad is the Mount Washington Cog Railway in New Hampshire,

378
00:32:46,440 --> 00:32:50,660
begun in 1865 by Sylvester Marsh and still running strong.

379
00:32:52,220 --> 00:32:55,540
Marsh was a successful and retired businessman who hatched an idea

380
00:32:55,541 --> 00:32:59,120
to build a railway to the 6,288-foot summit.

381
00:33:01,120 --> 00:33:04,220
The New Hampshire legislature thought Marsh was a lunatic.

382
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:08,320
When he asked them for a charter to build the railroad, they laughed at him.

383
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:11,320
He was very often called Crazy Marsh.

384
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:16,120
But when they discovered that he wasn't asking for any appropriation of money,

385
00:33:17,300 --> 00:33:22,420
they said, Sylvester, if you want to build that railroad with your own money and waste it,

386
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:23,780
you go right ahead.

387
00:33:23,781 --> 00:33:28,700
It will even include an amendment so that you may continue it onto the moon.

388
00:33:30,820 --> 00:33:35,700
Undaunted by the legislature's lunar insult, Marsh went to work on a rack railway,

389
00:33:36,460 --> 00:33:39,980
a cogged strip that would grip a giant mobile cog wheel.

390
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:47,540
Whether used to push, as they are here, or to tow, as they do in the Panama Canal,

391
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:50,980
these cogs are the defining feature of each railway.

392
00:33:50,981 --> 00:33:59,980
Well, that cogwheel in the middle of the track, with its heavy teeth, plays into that rack,

393
00:33:59,981 --> 00:34:04,980
and pin by pin by tooth by pin, claws its way up the hill.

394
00:34:04,981 --> 00:34:06,980
Everything is on that cogwheel.

395
00:34:06,981 --> 00:34:13,980
It not only propels the train up the mountain, but it offers a braking surface on the way down.

396
00:34:13,981 --> 00:34:22,800
though the concept wasn't original designing one that could climb a mountain was unprecedented

397
00:34:22,801 --> 00:34:29,340
marsh faced grades over 37 percent so he needed a giant cog to grip a cogged rat

398
00:34:29,341 --> 00:34:37,560
but before marsh settled in a cog railway to take him to the summit of Mount Washington

399
00:34:37,561 --> 00:34:43,860
he considered a more traditional towing option the funicular railway which

400
00:34:43,861 --> 00:34:46,380
uses cables to haul rail cars up inclines.

401
00:34:47,840 --> 00:34:52,280
But a chain as long as one Marsh would have needed to reach the summit would snap under

402
00:34:52,281 --> 00:34:55,580
its own weight, let alone the weight of cars full of people.

403
00:34:56,240 --> 00:34:57,420
So Marsh rejected it.

404
00:34:59,500 --> 00:35:04,900
But a funicular railway has been useful on shorter inclines, like Lookout Mountain, Tennessee.

405
00:35:06,460 --> 00:35:08,760
Funicular means a fixed system railway.

406
00:35:09,340 --> 00:35:13,080
On this particular one, there's two cars, one on each end of the cable.

407
00:35:13,860 --> 00:35:16,280
There's two double steel cables on the incline.

408
00:35:16,940 --> 00:35:19,420
One of them leaves one car, comes to the top of the mountain,

409
00:35:19,720 --> 00:35:21,860
routes two and a half times around the large drum,

410
00:35:22,420 --> 00:35:24,840
then drops back out to the other car.

411
00:35:27,700 --> 00:35:29,660
A little bit like a slanted elevator,

412
00:35:30,400 --> 00:35:33,960
the funicular railway relies on a clever but simple balancing mechanism.

413
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:39,920
Each of the two cars attached to the cable acts as a balance for the other

414
00:35:39,921 --> 00:35:44,000
so that the descending car, in effect, tows the ascending car.

415
00:35:45,320 --> 00:35:47,140
Energy is needed to power the pulley,

416
00:35:47,540 --> 00:35:51,680
but the system only needs enough to overcome the difference in weight between the cars,

417
00:35:52,220 --> 00:35:55,800
in other words, the passengers, and any inherent friction in the system.

418
00:35:56,980 --> 00:35:58,600
The wheels act merely as a guide.

419
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:08,500
Lookout Mountain attains a 72.7% grade at one point on its nearly one-mile run,

420
00:36:08,501 --> 00:36:10,640
the steepest of any funicular railway.

421
00:36:12,120 --> 00:36:15,980
Although the Lookout Mountain funicular has been towing people since 1895,

422
00:36:17,260 --> 00:36:20,640
manually powered funiculars have been around for at least 600 years.

423
00:36:21,480 --> 00:36:24,400
They're similar to the old-fashioned handheld ski lifts,

424
00:36:25,020 --> 00:36:27,960
which also use cables and a pulley to tow people up mountains.

425
00:36:29,800 --> 00:36:34,160
And their modern counterparts include the familiar gondolas at winter mountain resorts.

426
00:36:34,161 --> 00:36:42,300
Funiculars, cog railways and roller coasters stretch the concept of towing far beyond that

427
00:36:42,301 --> 00:36:44,300
of the body shop wrecker.

428
00:36:44,301 --> 00:36:52,900
They pull, push, haul and lug to get things from one place on Earth to another.

429
00:36:52,901 --> 00:36:56,200
But next, what about towing things off the Earth?

430
00:36:56,201 --> 00:36:59,160
How about towing a rocket into space?

431
00:36:59,161 --> 00:37:04,940
4% of the world's shipping fleet cannot navigate the Panama Canal.

432
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:08,600
The tow trains are strong enough to pull any ship on Earth.

433
00:37:09,260 --> 00:37:13,380
But some vessels are too big for the 110-foot-wide locks.

434
00:37:14,200 --> 00:37:16,320
Towing will return on Modern Marvelous.

435
00:37:21,940 --> 00:37:23,840
Towing doesn't need to be complicated.

436
00:37:24,560 --> 00:37:28,520
It can be as simple as a ball mount on the back of a pickup truck in a Class 1 hitch.

437
00:37:29,160 --> 00:37:36,160
This method is how most people tow their mobile homes and horse trailers, motorcycle platforms,

438
00:37:36,161 --> 00:37:38,160
and earthly possessions.

439
00:37:38,161 --> 00:37:46,160
But what if you want to move your immobile home, or for that matter, a 125-year-old church,

440
00:37:46,161 --> 00:37:50,160
one that sits on a foundation and weighs tons?

441
00:37:50,161 --> 00:37:55,160
We place wide flange steel beams perpendicular to the floor joist in the building.

442
00:37:55,161 --> 00:37:55,861
building.

443
00:37:55,862 --> 00:38:00,520
Then we set the hydraulic jacks under the beams and lift the house hydraulically.

444
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:05,360
After the house is lifted up, the dollies that are manufactured, especially for the

445
00:38:05,361 --> 00:38:07,900
house moving industry, we mount it on the dollies.

446
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:16,180
Redevelopment of the original site demanded the move, as parishioners decided to bring

447
00:38:16,181 --> 00:38:19,320
their old house of worship to their new site.

448
00:38:19,321 --> 00:38:22,740
Obviously, when moving a structure like a church,

449
00:38:22,741 --> 00:38:24,620
a large truck is necessary.

450
00:38:27,080 --> 00:38:29,040
Here in Simi Valley, California,

451
00:38:29,740 --> 00:38:33,640
master house movers filed permits for permission to move the church down the street.

452
00:38:34,960 --> 00:38:37,760
The street had to be measured to make sure it was wide enough.

453
00:38:38,980 --> 00:38:42,800
Once an acceptably wide route is picked, a time is chosen for the move.

